    "turn_text": "much fun. Oh my gosh, you think about it. Sort of, sort of, the cell phone kind of killed all that. Killed the film industry. Yeah, it really did. You don't see cameras anymore. My friend Hugh says, we shouldn't even really be talking about them as cell phones. The phone aspect of the cell phone is the least used aspect of the cell phone. Is that fair to say? We use the phone, the cell phone, smartphone, for almost everything but conversation. Don't we? I mean, I suppose it is our default mode of conversation for telephones. Not a lot of people have landlines anymore, I don't think. But we use it more for, I think, cameras and apps and web browsing and texting and things like that. It's a funny thing. Also, we need to get rid of the phrase but dial. When you accidentally call someone from your smartphone, it's not that. There's no dialing. We need to really fix the language here, David. This is my campaign. Fixing the language. No cell phone at any point in its history ever had a dial on it. It was never a rotary. We should not call it a, you know, what dial? It should be something else, but not that. I'm just here trying to fix the country.",
